Balanitis
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Erythematous swollen glans, Thick white subpreputial discharge, Localized pruritus/burning

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-006
Reason for Visit: Severe, acute penile pain and inability to retract the foreskin, requiring urgent assessment to rule out paraphimosis and provide immediate symptomatic relief.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an acutely swollen, exquisitely tender glans with foul-smelling discharge and localized urinary retention due to swelling. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to exclude ischemic paraphimosis and to initiate therapy to prevent further localized tissue damage before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The tip of my penis is incredibly swollen, red, and burning, and it hurts far too much to even try and pull the skin back."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms began rapidly over the past 48 hours. The patient describes an increasingly erythematous, swollen, and exquisitely tender glans penis. This is accompanied by intense itching, burning, and severe irritation localized to the glans and foreskin. A thick, white, clumpy discharge has been accumulating under the prepuce, with foul-smelling smegma noted. The localized oedema has resulted in an inability to retract the foreskin. Dysuria is present, secondary to inflammation extending to the urethral meatus. They report a recent course of broad-spectrum antibiotics for a sinus infection, which likely precipitated this fungal overgrowth.
Functional Status: Independent, though ambulation is limited due to friction-induced pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High distress and anxiety regarding the severe genital swelling; lives alone.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.1C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, anxious, adjusting posture frequently to avoid pressure on the groin.
Physical Exam: Genitourinary: Examination reveals an erythematous swollen glans with associated severe prepuce oedema. Retraction of the foreskin is impossible due to the swelling. A thick white subpreputial discharge is visible at the meatus. Severe localized pruritus and burning are reported upon gentle palpation. Expected investigation results would include direct microscopic visualization of pseudohyphae and budding yeast on potassium hydroxide preparation of the subpreputial discharge.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment; partial courses of previous antibiotics visible in the bathroom cabinet.
Medication Reconciliation: Recently completed a 10-day course of amoxicillin-clavulanate. No current active medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Balanitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Paraphimosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the foreskin being in the unretracted position, without the characteristic constricting band proximal to the glans.

Sexually Transmitted Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to the classic thick white clumpy discharge and recent antibiotic use strongly favoring a candidal etiology over chlamydia or gonorrhea.

Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the lack of spreading erythema up the penile shaft and absence of systemic fever.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, highly distressing exacerbation of balanitis resulting in severe local oedema and functional phimosis. The rapid onset of exquisite tenderness and inability to retract the foreskin raised a significant clinical index of suspicion for paraphimosis or impending urinary obstruction. Urgent physical examination was mandatory to definitively exclude ischemic complications and to provide immediate localized therapy. Delaying this assessment until regular in-hours consultation would have unnecessarily prolonged severe pain and increased the risk of secondary bacterial superinfection or structural tissue damage.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Commenced topical clotrimazole 1% cream applied to the affected area twice daily for 14 days. Provided oral fluconazole 150mg stat dose for rapid systemic antifungal effect. Prescribed a short course of oral NSAIDs for inflammation and analgesia.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maintain proper local hygiene without causing further irritation, the patient is advised to wash the area daily with warm water only, avoiding all soaps and shower gels. Noticing a reduction in burning and redness after stopping chemical irritants will positively reinforce this gentle washing habit.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to ensure swelling has reduced enough to allow safe foreskin retraction.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate medical review or Emergency Department presentation is required if the patient becomes completely unable to pass urine, develops a high fever, or if a red streak begins spreading up the shaft of the penis.

